 "s9_group_result": {
  "value": null,
  "display": "Passing all three: Hispanic-majority tracts and the lowest income quintile are under-assessed, assessed and taxed below their market-value share. Hispanic-majority tracts: persistent (the tier-adjusted sales check holds in 4 of 4 earlier rolls); a typical home (assessed $158,300) pays about $111 a year less, $1.7 million across 14,476 homes, and the fixed levy puts about $15 a year more on each of the other 106,991. The lowest income quintile: a 2025 result (the tier-adjusted sales check holds in 2 of 4 earlier rolls); a typical home (assessed $114,400) pays about $65 a year less, $1.7 million across 23,609 homes, and the fixed levy puts about $17 a year more on each of the other 97,858. The groups overlap, so the amounts do not add. White-majority tracts and the highest income quintile fail only the tier-specific time adjustment: consistent with assessments lagging faster appreciation in some areas, not a valuation pattern. Black-majority tracts: the two checks conflict (the bias-corrected shift, +1.1% [+0.6%, +1.6%], says over-assessed; the 2025 sales check, -1.3% [-2.4%, -0.4%], says under-assessed; both intervals exclude zero), so no claim is made.",
